# Whose costs count? The perspective question decides the answer
next: {"title":"What is a QALY really claiming about you?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/what-is-a-qaly-really-claiming-about-you.cast"}

## One Drug, Two Ledgers
voice: male
level: basic
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    y_label: Incremental cost (kr)
    x_label: Incremental effect (QALYs)
    wtp_threshold: 275000
    quadrant_labels: false
    points:
      - {label: Health-payer view, effect: 3, cost: 12000000, emphasize: true}
      - {label: Societal view, effect: 3, cost: 9000000}

    text claim_a "One drug, one verdict" #b5482e x 265 y 712 font_size 24 hidden true
    mark strike_a target claim_a kind strike hidden true
    text claim_b "One drug, two verdicts" #2f6b8f x 735 y 712 font_size 24 hidden true
    mark box_b target claim_b kind box hidden true
    label lambda_label "λ ≈ 275 000 kr per QALY" below #8f887c attach_to wtp_line hidden true
    arrow shift_arrow pt_0 -> pt_1 #d0865f hidden true
    label shift_label "Costs moved off the health budget" right #d0865f attach_to shift_arrow hidden true

Over these four parts we'll see that a technology's verdict can turn on a choice made before any evidence is read: whose ledger we write the costs in.
    draw claim_a

On the left, the intuition — the trial settles it. On the right, the uncomfortable possibility that the same drug earns two honest verdicts.
    draw claim_b

    draw x_axis y_axis parallel true

Effect sideways, cost upward; below this dashed line a technology buys health at Norway's reference price of roughly 275 000 kroner per good life-year.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label lambda_label

Take nusinersen for spinal muscular atrophy: in a stylized lifetime estimate, three QALYs gained for twelve million kroner of drug and hospital costs.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

Four million kroner per QALY — so far above the line that no reasonable threshold rescues it.
    point at.ref pt_0 gesture tap

Beslutningsforum said no, then yes for patients under eighteen once the price came down — the drug worked all along; the ledger was the fight.
    highlight pt_0 effect glow

    pause 0.4

Now change one thing only: ask not what the health budget pays, but what Norway pays.
    draw shift_arrow

Parents back at work, fewer hours of informal care, less municipal help and transport — say three million kroner over a lifetime — and the point drops straight down.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1 shift_label

Same child, same trial, same three QALYs: four million per QALY becomes three, and nothing about the medicine changed.
    point at.ref pt_1 gesture circle

    quiz "Moving to the societal ledger, what happened to ΔE, the QALYs gained?" intro "A quick check."
        right: ΔE is unchanged — perspective decides which costs are counted, not how much health is gained.
        wrong: Only the cost side moved; the health gain measured in the trial is the same either way.
        * It rose
        + It was unchanged
        * It fell

Here the drop changes no decision — three million still dwarfs the line — but for the quiet, high-volume technologies that sit near it, that same quarter is the whole difference between yes and no.
    highlight wtp_line effect glow delivery grave

Keep both points, and follow the money that made the lower one possible.
    clear keep ["x_axis","y_axis","wtp_line","wtp_label","lambda_label","pt_0","pt_label_0","pt_1","pt_label_1","claim_a","claim_b"]

The hospital never sees it: NAV carries the sick pay, the municipality the home care, the family the hours — the hospital still signs for twelve million.
    focus pt_0 pt_label_0

    quiz "Why doesn't the societal saving relieve the hospital's budget?"
        right: The money is real, but it accrues to NAV, municipal care and family time — never to the account that buys the drug.
        wrong: The saving is real enough; it simply lands in budgets the hospital does not hold.
        * The saving is speculative
        + It lands in other budgets: NAV, the municipality, the family
        * It arrives only after the patent expires

So the intuition fails — the arithmetic never contradicted itself, the frame around it did.
    draw strike_a

Two honest ledgers, two verdicts: picking the ledger is already picking whose costs count, and whose quietly don't.
    draw box_b

## The productivity argument and its critics
voice: male
level: basic
domain: {"x":[0,1],"y":[0,300000]}

    text claim_a "A: \"Production gained is just another cost item.\"" x 500 y 470 font_size 36 hidden true
    mark strike_a target claim_a kind strike hidden true
    text claim_b "B: \"Counting it prices patients by their wages.\"" x 500 y 330 font_size 36 hidden true
    axes ax x_label "ΔE (QALYs gained)" y_label "ΔC (kr)" hidden true
    curve thresh dashed #8f887c expr 275000*x hidden true
    label label_thresh "λ ≈ 275 000 kr/QALY" above #8f887c attach_to thresh hidden true
    dot p_payer #b5482e at.x 0.5 at.y 200000 hidden true
    label label_payer "Health payer: 400 000 kr/QALY" right #b5482e attach_to p_payer hidden true
    dot p_soc58 #2f6b8f at.x 0.5 at.y 110000 hidden true
    arrow arr_prod p_payer -> p_soc58 #2f6b8f hidden true
    label label_prod "− 90 000 kr production regained" left #2f6b8f attach_to arr_prod hidden true
    label label_soc58 "Societal, age 58: 220 000" right #2f6b8f attach_to p_soc58 hidden true

Now that we have widened the frame item by item, one item does most of the fighting — the patient's return to work — and one camp says it is nothing special, just another resource line.
    draw claim_a

The other camp answers that counting it quietly prices patients by their wages, and decides who gets treated.
    draw claim_b

Both sound reasonable, so let's settle it with one operation and one plane.
    clear delivery brisk

    draw ax

The dashed line is our threshold, lambda — around 275,000 kroner per QALY at low severity; above it we displace more health than we create.
    draw thresh label_thresh

    pause 0.3

Take a rehabilitation package after hip replacement, in stylised numbers: 200,000 kroner of hospital resources for half a QALY — an ICER of 400,000, sitting well above the line.
    draw p_payer label_payer

    pause 0.3

Our patient is 58 and goes back to work: 90,000 kroner of production regained, subtracted from the numerator, and the very same operation drops under the threshold at 220,000.
    draw arr_prod label_prod p_soc58 label_soc58

    quiz "Adding the return to work changed which part of the ICER?" intro "A quick check."
        right: It moved ΔC: the health gain is untouched, so the dot fell straight down.
        wrong: The dot moved vertically only — nobody gained extra health from the wage.
        * ΔE — the QALYs gained
        + ΔC — the numerator
        * The threshold λ

The first objection is double counting: if the people who valued this health state were already imagining lost income, the QALY has priced that loss once already.
    highlight arr_prod effect glow

The second is arithmetic — Koopmanschap's friction-cost method assumes a replacement worker arrives within months, so only a fraction of the production counts, and the dot climbs back above the line.
    dot p_friction #8a5fa8 at.x 0.5 at.y 175000
    label label_friction "Friction cost: 350 000" left #8a5fa8 attach_to p_friction

    pause 0.4

The third is the sharpest: same operation, same half QALY, but the patient is 75 and retired — there is nothing to subtract, so the dot never moves.
    label label_75 "Age 75, retired: still 400 000" above attach_to p_payer

Identical need, identical health gain, opposite verdicts — and the thing that differs is a wage, not a QALY.
    highlight p_payer effect glow delivery grave

    quiz "Two patients, same operation, same half QALY. Why does only the younger one pass?"
        right: Only his return to work subtracts from the numerator; the health gain is identical.
        wrong: The health gain and the hospital's bill are the same — what differs is the production counted in ΔC.
        * He gains more health
        + His wages offset ΔC
        * His operation is cheaper

That is why Norway's priority-setting framework declines to let production gains weigh on the verdict — the losers from that choice are where this series ends.
    focus p_payer label_75

    clear

So which of our two claims survived the figure?
    show claim_a claim_b

Not the first: including production is never neutral bookkeeping, because it re-ranks patients whose health gain is exactly the same.
    draw strike_a

Whose costs count is not an accounting detail — it decides which patients the analysis is even able to favour.

## Choosing a perspective in Norway — and naming the losers
voice: male
level: basic
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    title: Same drug, three ledgers
    x_label: ΔE (QALYs gained)
    y_label: ΔC (kroner)
    wtp_threshold: 1000000
    quadrant_labels: false
    points:
      - {label: ' ', effect: 6, cost: 5400000}
      - {label: ' ', effect: 6, cost: 8000000}
      - {label: ' ', effect: 6, cost: 7200000, emphasize: true}

    mark strike_soc target lab_soc kind strike hidden true
    mark box_guide target lab_ext kind box hidden true
    box p_narrow "Hospital budget only" x 260 y 600 hidden true
    box l_muni "Municipalities and families" #b5482e x 740 y 600 hidden true
    edge e1 p_narrow -> l_muni #8f887c hidden true
    box p_soc "Full societal base case" x 260 y 430 hidden true
    box l_prod "Patients who don't produce" #b5482e x 740 y 430 hidden true
    edge e2 p_soc -> l_prod #8f887c hidden true
    box p_ext "Extended health-payer" x 260 y 260 hidden true
    box l_displaced "The displaced patients" #b5482e x 740 y 260 hidden true
    edge e3 p_ext -> l_displaced #8f887c hidden true
    text lab_hosp "Hospital budget" x 700 y 630 font_size 20 hidden true
    text lab_ext "Extended health-payer" #8a5fa8 x 672 y 594 font_size 20 hidden true
    text lab_soc "Societal ledger" x 714 y 549 font_size 20 hidden true

Now that we have seen the productivity argument and the case against it, one question is left: which ledger should a Norwegian decision actually open?
    draw x_axis y_axis title

Effect gained runs across, extra cost runs up, and this dashed line is the threshold lambda — say one million kroner per QALY for a condition this severe; below it is a buy.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label

    pause 0.3

Some say: count every krone wherever it falls. On our stylized Spinraza figures that is six QALYs for five point four million net — nine hundred thousand per QALY, under the line.
    draw pt_0 lab_soc

Others say: a hospital can only spend what is in its own budget. The same six QALYs then cost eight million — one point three million per QALY, well above it.
    draw pt_1 lab_hosp

Same drug, same children, same effect, two opposite verdicts — decided by bookkeeping rather than by evidence.
    highlight pt_0 pt_1 effect glow

    quiz "What actually differs between those two points?"
        intro: A quick check before we resolve it.
        right: Only the cost side moved — ΔE is identical, so the perspective alone flipped the verdict.
        wrong: The effect and the threshold are the same in both — only the cost side changed.
        * The QALYs gained
        + Which costs were counted
        * The threshold lambda

    pause 0.4

Norway takes neither pole: the base case uses an extended health-payer perspective — the health service plus municipal care — which lands here, at seven point two million, or one point two million per QALY.
    draw pt_2 lab_ext

This is the number a Norwegian decision starts from, because the criterion is ressursbruk, resource use — what the health service gives up, not what society gains.
    draw box_guide

So the fully societal figure is struck from the base case; the priority-setting white paper was explicit that expected production gains must not decide who gets treated.
    draw strike_soc delivery grave

It is not thrown away, though — it comes back as a sensitivity analysis, printed beside the base case so the decision-maker can see what the wider frame would have said.
    highlight pt_0 effect glow

    quiz "Where do productivity gains belong in a Norwegian submission?"
        right: They are shown separately as a sensitivity analysis, so they inform without deciding.
        wrong: They are excluded from the base case, but still reported — as a sensitivity analysis.
        * In the base-case ICER
        + In a sensitivity analysis beside it
        * Nowhere at all

    erase lab_soc lab_hosp lab_ext strike_soc box_guide

So the perspective is settled. But every perspective is also a decision about who is allowed to be invisible.
    clear

Count only the hospital's budget, and a drug that sends a child home looks cheap — the municipality's nurses and the family's unpaid hours were never in the sum.
    draw p_narrow l_muni e1

Count everything, and the weight drifts toward patients who earn: the retired, the disabled and the not-yet-working become the cheapest people to leave untreated.
    draw p_soc l_prod e2

And Norway's middle path has its own quiet loser — the budget is fixed, so every krone approved displaces care for patients nobody ever names.
    draw p_ext l_displaced e3

    quiz "Under a narrow hospital-budget perspective, who absorbs the costs that disappear from the sum?"
        right: The care does not vanish; it moves to municipal services and to unpaid relatives, outside the ledger being read.
        wrong: The care still happens — it is simply paid for by municipalities and families instead.
        * Nobody — they are genuine savings
        + Municipalities and families
        * The manufacturer

Across these four parts: perspective is not a technical setting — it decides which costs are real, and every choice hands the bill to someone who is not in the room.
    highlight l_muni l_prod l_displaced effect glow

## Where next
end_page: true

